WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.304
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.155 --> 00:00:08.780
EVAN JONES: Hi.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.460
I'm Evan Jones
with Google Cloud,

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.740
and I'm really excited
to have Joy with us here.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.650
She is a data educator and a
supervisor at the Children's

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:16.910
Hospital of Philadelphia.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.283
Welcome to the show, Joy.

00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:19.200
JOY PAYTON: Thank you.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.205
It's great to be here.

00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:21.830
EVAN JONES: Yeah,
it's really exciting.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.690
A lot of things are happening
virtually these days,

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.990
and interviews are
just one of them,

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.690
but thankfully, we both
have really good internet

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:27.278
connections.

00:00:27.278 --> 00:00:29.570
And we've got a treasure
trove of questions to ask you,

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.030
particularly about certification
and your background.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.410
Do you mind if we just jump in?

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:34.577
JOY PAYTON: Yeah, fantastic.

00:00:34.577 --> 00:00:35.180
Let's go.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.097
EVAN JONES: All right.

00:00:36.097 --> 00:00:37.610
Tell me a little
bit about yourself

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.130
and what brought you
to this certification,

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.662
and how it's related to your
work, if you don't mind.

00:00:42.662 --> 00:00:43.370
JOY PAYTON: Sure.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.820
So as you stated, I work
for the Children's Hospital

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:47.760
of Philadelphia.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.290
And we are a customer of
Google Cloud platform,

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:54.380
as well as other
cloud providers.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:58.220
And two years ago, I was
sent to Google Cloud Next,

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.150
which was a great opportunity.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.530
and I really enjoyed it.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.420
And as part of the
lead-up to the conference,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.690
I received an email
inviting me to consider

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:12.920
applying for this diversity
cohort that was intended

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.510
to help broaden the appeal and
broaden the class of people

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:22.010
who were certified as
Google Cloud experts.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.480
And that was something
that really appealed to me.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.140
And it appealed to me
because, number one,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:30.020
for my own professional
growth and education,

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.042
I thought that would
be very useful for me--

00:01:32.042 --> 00:01:33.000
EVAN JONES: Absolutely.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.910
JOY PAYTON: --yeah,
as well as the fact

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.780
that I am a data educator, and
it's really important to me

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.310
to multiply everything that
I'm able to do and teach

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.860
others and deputize them, in
turn, to train still others.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.100
So it just seemed like
it was a real opportunity

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.060
to, in my local community,
at the Children's Hospital

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.880
of Philadelphia and
beyond, to really grow

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.188
some education around
cloud computing.

00:01:58.188 --> 00:02:00.230
EVAN JONES: What would
you say to those folks who

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:03.230
hear the word examination
or certificate,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.660
and they might not have a
strong background in data

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.940
or have the technical
skills, but they look at you

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.552
and they say, you know, if
she can do it, I can do it,

00:02:10.552 --> 00:02:11.510
let's go do this thing.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:12.385
I'm going to sign up.

00:02:12.385 --> 00:02:15.463
I'm going to be the next
cohort that's coming out, Joy.

00:02:15.463 --> 00:02:16.880
What would you say
to those people

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.130
who have a little bit
of-- at Google, we

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.980
call it imposter syndrome,
which is you walk into a room,

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.850
and you feel like
everyone else in the room

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.242
is smarter than you,
has done all this,

00:02:26.242 --> 00:02:28.200
and they deserve to be
here, and you might not.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.170
You feel like you
don't belong there,

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.162
even though absolutely, with
everything of their core being,

00:02:33.162 --> 00:02:35.120
they need to be there
and they should be there.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.290
How would you
motivate those people

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.460
that are going to be joining
that next cohort after you?

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:39.630
What would you say to them?

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.290
JOY PAYTON: Well, I
have two sound bites.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.080
One is, don't believe
everything you think.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.790
EVAN JONES: I like that one.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:49.130
JOY PAYTON: So just
because you think

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:52.220
this is beyond your
skills or you're

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.590
too far behind
because you're over 50

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.750
or you never worked
with computers,

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.490
or whatever the reason is, don't
believe everything you think.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.290
And also, if you're the
smartest person in the room,

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.680
you are in the wrong room.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:07.940
EVAN JONES: I love that one.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.060
JOY PAYTON: You need to be
with people that are smarter

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.030
than you and know more
than you, and start

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.470
being comfortable with
being uncomfortable.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.620
I think that's something
in this current--

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:21.950
this is being filmed during
the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.590
and a lot of us are getting
comfortable with being

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.460
uncomfortable.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.450
And that's a real life lesson.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.570
So I will say that imposter
syndrome I don't think ever

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:37.230
really goes away, but I have
learned to talk back to it.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.070
And one of the
things that I find--

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:43.190
because I've taught technology
to children and adults,

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.050
and one of the things
that I've noticed

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:48.690
is the emergence of
the confidence gap.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.570
And in my experience,
this happens

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:56.300
around fifth or sixth
grade, where some people,

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.840
predominantly boys,
although not exclusively,

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.410
feel very confident
in math and science,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.300
and when they run
across challenges,

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.210
like a really hard
math problem-- so math

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:11.150
starts getting really hard
around sixth, seventh grade,

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.622
right?

00:04:12.622 --> 00:04:14.330
When they run across
a hard math problem,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.029
they think to themselves,
wow, this is a hard problem.

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:20.730
I am super smart for
tackling this hard problem.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.850
And then there's a group
of students, predominantly,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.320
but not exclusively
girls, who maybe,

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.020
because they've done well
in school up to this point

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:34.940
without as much effort, realize
that, wow, this is much more

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.010
effort than I'm accustomed to.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:40.170
I must not be up to the task.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:45.270
And that confidence gap I still
continue to see in people.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.150
And it's interesting
to me because I work

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:49.580
with very, very smart people.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:52.760
I work with people
who are MD/PhDs.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.830
I work with students
who are in grad school

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:57.160
at Yeshiva, who are
adult learners who

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.715
have important full-time jobs.

00:04:59.715 --> 00:05:03.050
I work with very
smart people, and they

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.400
have imposter syndrome.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.690
And they feel like,
wow, this is hard.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.762
And I wrote some
code, and it failed.

00:05:09.762 --> 00:05:11.720
And what they don't
realize because they're not

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.550
in this industry is, in
tech, you fail all the time.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.320
You fail and you
fail and you fail.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.350
And you finally succeed, but
you don't get to rest in it

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:22.940
and roll around in
how wonderful it

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.940
is because you've got to
go on to the next thing

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:26.790
and start failing again.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:29.610
And that's not like most jobs.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:33.200
So I do a lot of soft
skills, and helping

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.540
people get comfortable
with failure

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.900
and get comfortable with
not being great at things.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.360
You're not going to be great
at this when you start.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.080
And that's fine.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.060
You're not supposed to be great.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.370
So hang in there,
you're going to be fine,

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.490
don't panic when
you make a mistake,

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:51.763
and don't believe
everything you think.

00:05:51.763 --> 00:05:53.430
EVAN JONES: I think
that's perfect, Joy.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.870
I love the get comfortable
being uncomfortable.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:58.790
Even to this day, I
mean, a lot of people

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.220
look at the folks
who work at Google

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.310
and, oh, you've got a job
at Google, that's like--

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.948
you know, everyone
there is a genius

00:06:06.948 --> 00:06:08.990
and writing the next
self-driving car application

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:10.970
or something like that.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:12.470
I can tell you,
every day, there's

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:13.680
a moment where I'm like--

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.830
you're running into
that same type of wall,

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:16.910
that same type of
wall where you're

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.650
in fifth grade or sixth
grade, and you're up

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:20.525
against a math problem
or a technical problem

00:06:20.525 --> 00:06:23.960
or an interpersonal problem,
and you feel a little bit

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.040
of that uncomfortability.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.860
And I couldn't +1 or
agree with your point more

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.640
of it's the step
that you take next.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:30.740
It's either you're going
to run away from that,

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.140
or you're going to say it's all
right to feel uncomfortable,

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:34.765
but I'm still going
to do this anyways.

00:06:34.765 --> 00:06:37.280
I think that's a perfect
message out there.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.130
All right, the certification.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:40.550
I would love to ask you a
few more questions on that,

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:41.180
if you don't mind.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:41.888
JOY PAYTON: Sure.

00:06:41.888 --> 00:06:45.350
EVAN JONES: How has the
certification itself either

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:47.840
impacted the
organization you work for

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.660
or the communities
that you're a part of?

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:51.530
JOY PAYTON: Sure.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:53.660
I mean, especially as
an adjunct professor,

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:56.570
it's very important to me that
the education that I'm giving

00:06:56.570 --> 00:07:00.060
my students is the
most cutting edge,

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.390
because it might take them
two, three, four years--

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.060
these are adult learners
who are very busy--

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:08.360
to finish their master's degree,
which means that, by the time

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.150
they leave, what I taught them
their first year is already

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:14.240
a couple of years
out of date, right?

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.250
So I want to make
sure that, the day I

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:19.250
deliver that lecture
or share that Colab

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:23.880
notebook, that it is the most
up-to-date I can possibly do.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:28.370
And so I do a lot of
advocating with senior folks

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.940
at my education institution,
at Yeshiva University,

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:35.340
for adopting cloud
platforms and saying,

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.390
this is where our students
are going to be working.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:41.930
So sure, they should
certainly install Python

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:44.790
on their laptops, absolutely.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:49.490
You know, if you want to have
a SQL server spun up someplace

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:51.890
on campus, that's great, too.

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:55.100
But realistically,
as far as job skills,

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.840
our students are going to
be working in the cloud.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.690
So how can we really leverage
my skills and whatever topic

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:06.920
I'm teaching, whether that's
learning to program in Python

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:12.350
or doing computer vision or
some sort of data engineering

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.030
or data pipelines.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:16.250
No matter what sort
of topic I'm teaching,

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:19.820
there's a way to tie it to
cloud in a way that really

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:24.230
is building students' abilities
to understand the vocabulary

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:26.840
to be able to be
autodidacts, right?

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:30.500
To have the terminology,
to know the concepts

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.510
well enough to know
what to search for.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:38.112
You know, I always say that
so much of what I teach

00:08:38.112 --> 00:08:40.070
is telling people what
to Google, and helping--

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:41.028
EVAN JONES: Absolutely.

00:08:41.028 --> 00:08:44.660
JOY PAYTON: --people learn
how to Google, right?

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.290
And I also tell
people, you know what,

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:48.150
I Google this every day, too.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:53.840
So if you think that I have
the syntax memorized, I do not.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:54.502
And--

00:08:54.502 --> 00:08:56.210
EVAN JONES: People
always get surprised--

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.183
so I do a lot of
BigQuery and SQL courses,

00:08:58.183 --> 00:08:59.600
and they always
get surprised when

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:00.680
I'm presenting in
the classroom and I'm

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.097
going to Google to look up,
like, some of these functions.

00:09:03.097 --> 00:09:06.413
They're like, wait a minute, you
presented on an online platform

00:09:06.413 --> 00:09:08.580
with ease and I'm like, I
don't memorize this stuff,

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:09.710
but I know where to look.

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:10.280
Absolutely.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:11.702
Couldn't agree more.

00:09:11.702 --> 00:09:13.160
JOY PAYTON: Yeah,
so I mean, that's

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.930
my life as an adjunct professor.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.330
I would also say,
I've had people

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:21.780
come to me at work or just in
the community and say, hey,

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:25.490
I saw on LinkedIn that you
are Google Cloud certified.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:27.770
I want to find out
more about this.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:30.800
And these are often
career changers or people

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.060
who discovered,
to their surprise,

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:37.490
that they really liked DevOps
or platform engineering

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:41.030
or some of the
background engineering.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:43.820
And maybe that wasn't the
job that they were hired for,

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:47.180
but they realized that, wow,
I have some technical skills

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:50.160
and abilities that
I didn't know about.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:53.450
And in fact, I had a
conversation with someone

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:57.770
who has a lot of information
science experience,

00:09:57.770 --> 00:09:59.730
but is not a programmer.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.940
She would not consider
herself a programmer.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.330
And she said, can I pursue this?

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.900
I said absolutely, right?

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:11.340
You know, there are specialty,
professional-grade exams

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:18.990
for applications developers,
but this initial associate-level

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:22.867
exam is just a great overview
of lots of different things.

00:10:22.867 --> 00:10:25.200
And I said, if nothing else,
what it will do is give you

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.560
a chance to learn a lot,
figure out what parts you like

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.810
and what parts you don't.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:33.330
For example, I am so
grateful that there

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:34.980
are network engineers.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:38.100
I thank my lucky stars
for them every day

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.710
because I could
not do their job.

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:41.520
Right?

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.250
EVAN JONES: I feel the same way.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:44.630
Any questions that
I ever get on,

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:47.610
like, IP addresses and routing,
like, private network traffic,

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.370
I'm like, ooh.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:53.130
JOY PAYTON: I mean, I look up
how do you write a CIDR block

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:54.652
notation, you know, probably--

00:10:54.652 --> 00:10:55.610
EVAN JONES: Every time.

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:56.020
Every time.

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:57.603
JOY PAYTON: --at
least once a quarter.

00:10:57.603 --> 00:11:00.580
You know, sometimes it'll stick
around for a little while.

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:02.590
So one of the things,
you know, I've

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:07.050
had a very circuitous
career path.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:11.580
And along the way, I took
10 years out of technology

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.980
to do full-time volunteer work.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:18.690
And so I worked in prisons and
schools and homeless shelters

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.300
in Spain and Bolivia
and El Salvador.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:23.700
And one of the things that
I really fell in love with

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.540
was the power of education
to change people's lives

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.820
at every level, right?

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:35.670
I worked with women who spoke
only their indigenous language,

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:40.080
did not speak Spanish, maybe
only spoke Quechua or Aymara

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.470
in Bolivia, and
realizing that just

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:46.920
their inability to speak
Spanish kept them out

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:48.720
of the marketplace.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:51.180
It kept them impoverished.

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:55.410
It kept them under the control,
sometimes, of domestic abusers.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:58.020
And all the way up
to people that I

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:01.560
saw in prison who were
getting degrees and making

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.120
their lives better.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:08.550
And so I've come back
to full-time work

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:11.170
because I love technology
and I love education,

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:13.770
and I'm lucky enough
to combine the two.

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.690
And at the end of
the day, what I

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:20.340
would love to do is go back
into some of these lower

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:24.000
resourced areas
and say, hey, NGOs,

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.160
I would like to help
you boost your signal.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:28.690
Let's take a look at your data.

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:31.470
How can you be in charge
of your data story?

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:33.300
How can I help you?

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.270
There are resources that
I can connect you with.

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:41.400
And not only can I help you,
NGO, but can I also help

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:42.890
the community you serve?

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:44.470
What's the population you serve?

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:45.960
What are they like?

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:47.970
Because the wonderful
thing about technology

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:52.900
is it's not the formal degrees
you have, it's what you can do.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:54.720
And this is one of
the few industries,

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:59.190
I feel like, that that
continues to be true for.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:04.800
And knowing that I have the
skills in Google Cloud Platform

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.060
to help other people
at all levels,

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:10.410
including people with
very low literacy,

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:13.440
computer literacy, that
they can access this stuff

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:14.370
through a URL.

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:16.390
They need a strong
internet connection,

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:19.150
which is not nothing,
that can be challenging,

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:24.330
but with just a strong internet
connection and a Chromebook

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:27.270
or a low-powered
laptop, I can really

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:32.190
help someone grow in skills that
will help them make a living,

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:34.260
help them provide
for their families,

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:38.640
and also improve the
lot of organizations

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:40.710
in under-resourced
areas that are doing

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:43.210
really important, good things.

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:43.710
So--

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:44.490
EVAN JONES: That's so--

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:44.960
JOY PAYTON: --that's my dream.

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:46.200
EVAN JONES: --well said.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:47.630
I love that dream.

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:50.610
And it's the dream
of Google as a whole.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.950
Their core mission is to
make the world's information

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:56.235
universally accessible
to anyone and everyone.

00:13:56.235 --> 00:13:58.380
You know, not just people
in the United States,

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:00.797
not just people of a certain
class or a certain privilege,

00:14:00.797 --> 00:14:01.380
as well.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:06.335
So it reminds me when you speak,
like the Chromebook and a URL

00:14:06.335 --> 00:14:07.710
and the internet,
and they access

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.775
one of your IPython
notebooks and they're

00:14:09.775 --> 00:14:12.150
doing a machine learning model
that would have previously

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:15.030
taken an entire Google data
center to do like 10 years ago,

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:16.920
now they're running on
their laptop in a cafe

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.087
or something like that, too.

00:14:20.217 --> 00:14:22.550
Being able to access that
technology, and to your point,

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:25.190
to be able to access the
educational resources

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:26.940
and those badges, like
the certifications,

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:30.130
as well, is just such
a powerful story, Joy.

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:32.800
Thank you so much for sharing
not only your background,

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:34.170
but just the clear passion.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:35.660
I thought I'm a
passionate person.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:37.380
Listening to you just
gets me motivated to take

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:39.172
even more of these
certifications, as well.

00:14:39.172 --> 00:14:40.510
I certainly don't have them all.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:42.150
So thank you so, so much.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:46.420
Any closing words from your
side to inspire the watchers?

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:47.895
JOY PAYTON: Oh, gosh.

00:14:47.895 --> 00:14:48.437
Let me think.

00:14:48.437 --> 00:14:48.550
Let me pause.

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:49.520
EVAN JONES: I put you
on the spot, I know.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:50.050
I'm sorry.

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:50.230
JOY PAYTON: You did.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:51.100
Let me think.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.280
I'm usually pretty
quick on my feet, too.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.265
EVAN JONES: No, I'll
say mine, and then we

00:14:56.265 --> 00:14:57.140
can close with yours.

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:58.723
So mine, at Google,
we have a moniker,

00:14:58.723 --> 00:15:01.330
and this is different than other
companies that I work with,

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:04.333
is good artists copy,
but great artists steal.

00:15:04.333 --> 00:15:06.250
And so one of my favorite
things you mentioned

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:08.650
is these collaborative
notebooks for machine learning.

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:10.840
A lot of our courses
that I put out,

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.630
I draw inspiration from a
lot of the other works that

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:14.680
are out there.

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.390
Data science in
particular is meant

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:19.390
to be a community of
folks kind of achieving

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:21.058
a goal or an objective together.

00:15:21.058 --> 00:15:22.850
So don't feel like you
need to go it alone,

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:24.580
especially if you're looking
at other people's resources,

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:25.250
as well.

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:26.890
And certainly,
internally at Google,

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:29.200
we're constantly stealing
each other's work

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:32.100
and making the insights
together, as well.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:36.880
So all right, close us with
a pithy example or quote.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:37.642
No pressure.

00:15:37.642 --> 00:15:39.850
JOY PAYTON: So I come at
this from a data scientist's

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:40.540
perspective.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:44.800
So other people may come to
GCP from a lot of interest

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:51.700
in high-performance computing
or sort of network resilience

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:55.170
or other issues or
areas of interest,

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.130
but I'm principally
a data scientist,

00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:00.020
so I'm going to give you the
answer of a data scientist.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:04.000
If you want to learn how to be
a data scientist because you

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:07.018
think this is interesting,
then you definitely

00:16:07.018 --> 00:16:08.560
need to be involved
with Google Cloud

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:11.410
Platform, because one of
the best ways to learn data

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:13.300
is using big data.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:16.840
And there is a great
set of resources for you

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.810
in Google Public data sets
in BigQuery, which is just

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:23.200
a great place to get started.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:26.020
There's tons of tutorials
that'll help you there

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:28.190
that are available at no cost.

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:30.580
The hosting of the data
sets is at no cost.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.920
And all the work that you need
to do can be done in free tier.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:39.100
So there are lots of ways that,
even without participating

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:42.780
in a formal training process
or signing up for an exam,

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.990
that you can grow your
skills and really find a data

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:47.600
set that you're
passionate about.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:50.620
I tell people, if you love
sports, look at sports data.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:52.990
If you're concerned
about hunger and poverty,

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:54.760
look for those data sets.

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:57.730
Find something you care
about, and you will definitely

00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:00.235
find some trends that
make you scratch your head

00:17:00.235 --> 00:17:03.730
or make you angry
or make you excited

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.359
or make you ask questions.

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:11.343
So find some data, work with
it, and change the world.

00:17:11.343 --> 00:17:12.260
EVAN JONES: I love it.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:13.940
That's such a
great way to close.

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:15.980
All right, we have
Joy with us from CHOP,

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:18.050
which is the Children's
Hospital of Philadelphia.

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:19.640
Thank you so much
for taking this time

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:21.444
to talk a little bit
about [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:21.444 --> 00:17:23.569
and your entire background
that's led you this far,

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:25.537
and carrying forward with
you for your students

00:17:25.537 --> 00:17:27.829
and adult learners that you're
taking forward, as well.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:29.330
Thank you so much.

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:30.750
JOY PAYTON: Thank you so
much for your time, as well.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:31.375
This was great.

00:17:31.375 --> 00:17:32.390
Be well.

